'''''Adiantum atroviride''''', commonly known as '''maidenhair fern''', is a plant in the family [[Pteridaceae]]. It is [[Endemism|endemic]] to Australia and occurs in the [[Northern Territory]], [[Queensland]] and [[New South Wales]].{{R|POWO}}

==Description==
''Adiantum atroviride'' is a small fern with a short creeping or somewhat erect [[rhizome]] with dark brown to black triangular scales. The fronds are about {{cvt|75|cm}} long, loosely clustered, with shiny black [[stipe (botany)|stipe]]s and [[rachis]]es.{{R|ATFL|FOA|PLANTNET}}

The laminae are 2- to 4-pinnate, the ultimate divisions are [[Glossary of leaf morphology#flabellate|flabellate]] (fan shaped), up tp {{cvt|13|mm|1}} long, with shallow lobes on the distal margin. There are between 1 and 7 round to kidney shaped sori on the distal margins, usually at the base of a deep narrow [[sinus (botany)|sinus]] between lobes.{{R|ATFL|FOA|PLANTNET}}

==Taxonomy==
''Adiantum atroviride'' was first described by the Australian botanist [[Peter Dundas Bostock]], and published in [[Flora of Australia (series)|Flora of Australia]] in 1998. Prior to that it was considered to be a variety of ''[[Adiantum aethiopicum|A. aethiopicum]]''.

===Etymology===
The genus name ''Adiantum'' comes from the Ancient Greek ''[[wikt:ἀδίαντον|adíantos]]'' meaning wet, and is a reference to the way the laminae shed water. The [[Botanical name#Binary name|species epithet]] ''atroviride'' is from the Latin ''ater'' for black, combined with ''viridis'' (green), referring to the black stipes and green foliage of the plant.{{R|NPSC}}

==Distribution and habitat==
This species occurs rarely in [[Arnhem Land]] in the Northern Territory, but is common on the east coast of Australia, from the [[Torres Strait]] all the way to southeastern [[New South Wales]]. It is a terrestrial fern inhabiting dry to medium dry vine forest and sclerophyll forest on a variety of soils.{{R|ATFL|FOA|PLANTNET}}

==Conservation==
This species is listed by Queensland's [[Department of Environment and Science (Queensland)|Department of Environment and Science]] as [[special least concern]],{{R|DESQLD}} a ranking that is unique to Queensland and is placed in between [[Least-concern_species|least concern]] and [[Near-threatened species|near threatened]].{{R|QCS}}

==Cultivation==
This is a popularly cultivated fern, valued for its ornamental qualities.{{R|ATFL}}

